WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
During the Yongle era, craftsmen began building Beijing's palace city on a grand scale.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.880
Busy figures were everywhere on the construction site, as everyone divided the work and cooperated with great energy.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.760
Thousands upon thousands of craftsmen brought in timber, bricks and stones, and glazed tiles.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.280
The heavy timber was lifted by everyone's combined strength, moving forward slowly step by step.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.600
Around the year 1420, the magnificent Forbidden City was basically completed.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.800
The golden roofs glittered in the sunlight, a truly spectacular sight.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.600
The palace of red walls and yellow tiles became the core place where the emperor lived.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.200
The emperor walked through the long corridor, looking at his new home with satisfaction.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.200
Here the emperor handled state affairs and received all the civil and military officials of the court.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.400
Ministers held their memorials and waited in line, and everything in the palace was in perfect order.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.400
The Forbidden City's grand layout became a representative of ancient palace architecture.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.600
Seen from above, row after row of red walls and golden tiles looked neat and majestic.
